{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_time",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "The query targets 'ticket types that create the heaviest agent workload.' This categorization is the ticket-type dimension the query centers on; it is intervenable in the sense that categories could be reduced/rerouted, and it is the named lever."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_system",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The affected system is a common cause of both what type/category a ticket is and how long it takes to resolve; not named in the query. It is however largely collinear with category, weakening its distinctness."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mode",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The nature of the failure drives both which category/workload bucket a ticket falls in and its resolution difficulty; not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "ticket_complexity_signal",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Complexity plausibly influences both which ticket types generate heavy workload and resolution time; not named in query. Borderline mediator, but as an intrinsic difficulty it can act as a common cause."
    },
    {
      "name": "location_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Scope (individual vs enterprise-wide) affects both ticket type/workload and resolution time; not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolver_match",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Constant (true for all 500 rows); provides no variation and cannot adjust for anything."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_indicator",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an issue recurs can be a common cause of both category/workload and resolution time; not named. Near-constant (480 true), so limited practical value."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_open_unresolved",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Being open/unresolved is downstream of (a function of) resolution time itself; conditioning on it is a collider/selection risk, not a confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["incident_category"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "affected_system", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4},
    {"name": "failure_mode", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.5},
    {"name": "ticket_complexity_signal", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6},
    {"name": "location_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55},
    {"name": "recurrence_indicator", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.45,
  "rationale": "A plausible treatment (incident_category / ticket type) is present and is intervenable, matching the 'reduce the ticket types that drive workload' lever. Several genuinely un-named confounders (failure_mode, ticket_complexity_signal, location_scope, affected_system) capture common causes of both ticket type and resolution time, which is what the what-if test rewards. However, quality is diminished: many treatment-adjacent columns are highly collinear with category (mediators/redundant), one column (resolver_match) is a useless constant, and is_open_unresolved is a collider that is outcome-derived and should not be conditioned on. The set sets up an interventional framing but the confounder adjustment is only moderately clean."
}
